In **February 1947** British Prime Minister **Clement Attlee** announced that Britain would leave India by **June 1948**. **Lord Louis Mountbatten** arrived as the last Viceroy in March 1947.

After talks with leaders, Mountbatten announced the **3 June Plan**:
- British India would be partitioned into **India and Pakistan**;
- the legislative assemblies of **Punjab and Bengal** would vote on whether to divide their provinces (both voted to partition);
- referendums in the **North-West Frontier Province** and **Sylhet** district of Assam;
- princely states could join either dominion;
- the date of transfer was brought forward to **15 August 1947**.

Congress leaders accepted the plan with deep reluctance, seeing it as the only way to avoid continuing violence and a weak, paralysed centre. Gandhi had opposed partition but did not lead an opposition to the Congress decision. **Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan** felt abandoned, as his province was now to go to Pakistan.

The British Parliament passed the **Indian Independence Act** in **July 1947**. The tight timetable — just over ten weeks — left little time to prepare for the division of an army, a civil service, railways, treasuries and even library books.

For decades, Partition was studied mainly through **high politics** — the negotiations of leaders and viceroys. Since the 1990s historians such as **Urvashi Butalia** (*The Other Side of Silence*), **Gyanendra Pandey** and **Yasmin Khan** have stressed the experiences of ordinary people, especially women, using **oral history** — interviews with survivors.

Different explanations are still debated:
- the role of British policy and the hasty timetable;
- the role of the Muslim League's demand and its leaders;
- the role of Congress decisions in 1937 and 1946;
- the role of communal organisations and local violence.

A good historian keeps several causes in view and listens to many voices. Partition is also remembered differently in India, Pakistan and Bangladesh. Museums like the **Partition Museum** in Amritsar (opened 2017) preserve objects and testimonies.

> Did you know? Many Partition refugees brought recipes with them — dishes now considered classics in Delhi's food culture are linked to Punjabi refugee families who set up eateries after 1947.

When the Mauryan empire broke up around 185 BCE, India did not fall into darkness. Quite the opposite: the next five centuries were among the most creative and connected in its history. New rulers came from Central Asia and the Deccan; trade linked India with Rome, China and Southeast Asia; Buddhism and new forms of Hinduism flourished; and art took on faces we still recognise.

The main powers, roughly in order:

- **Shungas** and **Kanvas** in Magadha and central India (c. 185–28 BCE);
- **Indo-Greeks** in the north-west, the most famous being **Menander** (Milinda), whose dialogue with the monk Nagasena is preserved in the Buddhist *Milindapanha*;
- **Shakas** (Scythians), whose western branch ruled Gujarat and Malwa for centuries;
- **Parthians** (Pahlavas) briefly in the north-west;
- **Kushanas**, a great Central Asian empire;
- **Satavahanas** in the Deccan;
- and finally the **Guptas** from the 4th century CE.

Historians once called parts of this time a 'dark age' between empires. That label has been dropped: coins, inscriptions, sculptures and trade goods show it was anything but dark.

> Did you know? The Indo-Greeks were the first rulers in India to issue coins with the king's portrait and name — a practice later rulers copied, which is why coins are so useful for dating this period.

The **Kushanas** were a branch of the Yuezhi people of Central Asia. By the 1st century CE they ruled from the Oxus river to the Ganga plains, with capitals at **Purushapura (Peshawar)** and **Mathura**.

Their greatest king, **Kanishka**, is often linked with the **Shaka era of 78 CE**, though historians debate his exact date (some place his accession later, around 127 CE). Kanishka:

- patronised Buddhism; the **Fourth Buddhist Council** was held in Kashmir under him, traditionally linked with the rise of **Mahayana** Buddhism;
- issued abundant **gold coins** showing Greek, Iranian, Indian and Buddhist deities: a sign of an empire of many peoples;
- supported scholars such as the poet **Ashvaghosha** (author of *Buddhacharita*) and, by tradition, the physician **Charaka**.

The Kushanas controlled a key stretch of the **Silk Route**, the network of caravan roads linking China to the Roman world. Chinese silk, Indian spices, gems, cotton and ivory flowed west; Roman gold flowed east. Kushana kings also took grand titles such as *devaputra* ('son of god'), perhaps borrowed from Chinese rulers, and set up statues of themselves in shrines at Mat near Mathura.

**Two great art schools** grew under Kushana rule:

- **Gandhara** (north-west): grey schist sculptures with Greco-Roman features, wavy hair and draped robes;
- **Mathura**: red sandstone figures with a distinctly Indian style, also producing Jain and Hindu images.

Both schools made some of the earliest known **images of the Buddha in human form**; before this, he had been shown only through symbols such as footprints, an empty seat or a wheel.

In the early 4th century CE, a new family rose in Magadha. **Chandragupta I** married a **Lichchhavi** princess, Kumaradevi, an alliance proudly shown on coins, took the title *Maharajadhiraja* ('great king of kings'), and began the **Gupta era in 319–320 CE**.

**Samudragupta** (c. 335–375 CE), his son, is known mostly from the **Allahabad Pillar inscription**, a prashasti composed by his court poet **Harishena** and carved on the same pillar as an Ashokan edict. It lists his campaigns:

- kings of **Aryavarta** (north India) were 'uprooted' and their lands annexed;
- twelve rulers of **Dakshinapatha** (the south and east coast) were defeated, captured and then **released** to rule as subordinates;
- forest chiefs (*atavika rajyas*) were made servants;
- frontier kingdoms and gana states paid tribute;
- distant rulers, including the king of Sri Lanka, sent gifts or sought favour.

Remember: this is a praise-poem, so the language is grand. But coins support the picture of a confident ruler. One type shows Samudragupta **playing the veena**, and the inscription praises him as a poet. Some modern writers called him 'the Napoleon of India', a phrase you will find in older books.

**Chandragupta II** (c. 375–415 CE), also called **Vikramaditya**, defeated the **Shakas** of western India, gaining Gujarat's ports and their sea trade. He issued silver coins there and gold coins elsewhere. His daughter **Prabhavati Gupta** married into the **Vakataka** dynasty of the Deccan and ruled as regent, extending Gupta influence south.

Key reform laws:
- **1829** — sati banned;
- **1856** — Hindu Widows' Remarriage Act;
- **1872** — Native Marriage Act;
- **1891** — **Age of Consent Act** raised the age of consent for girls from 10 to 12, after public outrage at the death of a child bride; **Bal Gangadhar Tilak** opposed it as foreign interference in custom, showing reform could clash with politics;
- **1929** — **Child Marriage Restraint Act** (Sarda Act) set the minimum age of marriage at 14 for girls and 18 for boys.

**Limits.** Most reformers were upper-caste, urban, English-educated men; laws often reached few people; widow remarriages remained rare for decades. Some reforms leaned on colonial laws, which critics saw as justifying British rule.

**Legacy.** The reformers created schools, colleges, newspapers and associations; women such as Savitribai, Ramabai and Rokeya became public voices; ideas of equality fed directly into the national movement and, in 1950, the Constitution's guarantees of equality and abolition of untouchability (Article 17).

In the mid-9th century, **Vijayalaya**, a chief of the old Chola family, captured **Thanjavur** around 850 CE from the Muttaraiyar chiefs. His successors built the largest and most powerful empire in South Indian history.

**Rajaraja I** (985–1014 CE):
- conquered the Cheras and Pandyas and northern **Sri Lanka**, and fought the Western Chalukyas;
- took the Maldives;
- built the magnificent **Brihadishvara (Rajarajeshvaram) temple** at Thanjavur, completed around **1010 CE**. Its tower (*vimana*) rises about 66 m, and the capstone is said to be a single granite block.

**Rajendra I** (1014–1044 CE), his son, went further:
- led an expedition to the **Ganga** in the north, after which he took the title **Gangaikonda Chola** ('the Chola who took the Ganga') and built a new capital, **Gangaikonda Cholapuram**, with a great temple and a reservoir;
- around **1025 CE**, launched a **naval expedition against Srivijaya**, a powerful kingdom of Sumatra and the Malay peninsula, which controlled the Straits of Malacca. Inscriptions name ports including Kadaram (Kedah). Historians think the aim was to protect Chola trade with China and Southeast Asia.

The Chola navy made the Bay of Bengal, for a time, almost a 'Chola lake'. Chola embassies went to the Song court in China.

Later rulers like **Kulottunga I** (1070–1122) joined the Chola and Eastern Chalukya lines. By the 13th century the Cholas declined against the Pandyas and Hoysalas.

The Rigvedic gods are mostly **forces of nature**:

- **Indra**, the most praised, wielder of the thunderbolt, who breaks the demon Vritra and releases the waters;
- **Agni**, fire, the messenger who carries offerings from humans to the gods;
- **Soma**, a sacred plant and its drink;
- **Varuna**, guardian of **rita**, the cosmic order;
- **Surya, Ushas** (the dawn), **Vayu** (wind), the **Maruts** (storm gods).

Worship centred on the **yajna**, the fire sacrifice. Ghee, grain and soma were offered into the fire while priests chanted. There were no temples or images; the altar was built for each ritual.

The early hymns ask for practical blessings: cattle, sons, rain, victory, long life. But some later hymns turn philosophical. The **Nasadiya Sukta** (Hymn of Creation) asks how the universe began and ends with a startling admission that perhaps even the one who looks down from the highest heaven does not know. It is a remarkable moment of doubt in a religious text.

> Did you know? The **Gayatri mantra**, still chanted daily by millions, is a Rigvedic verse addressed to Savitr, the sun.
